# EVERY `between` AS FILED, AS A CROSS-DOCUMENT REFERENCE. One row per reference: which layer of
# which OTHER document a composer says the double counting runs against.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE MODEL HAS TWO `pm:ForeignId` REFERENCES AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS NAMED AS ONE. A
#    `asrt:Part` has `composition/part_references.sqlc`; `asrt:Elimination/between` had only the
#    raw table, read by a single observation, filed under EVIDENCE. So anyone enumerating what
#    this model points at in other documents composed the parts and got half the answer, and the
#    BPMN mapping did exactly that: it reasoned from `elimination`, a magnitude BPMN genuinely
#    does not need, and `between` inherited a refusal that was never about it.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE REASON IT WAS NEVER WRITTEN IS STRUCTURAL, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. This tree's pressure
#   is *a rule reads only what it needs, so a field no rule reads is under no pressure to exist*.
#   `between` is the ONE cross-document reference that no rule MAY check: the schema licenses an
#   unresolvable one outright, because *a group eliminating against a member that files nothing,
#   or against a layer folded into a different composed layer, is ordinary*. So no rule could pull
#   it into view, and nothing else did.
#
# ⭐⭐ SO ITS COMPANION IS AN OBSERVATION AND NOT A CHECK. `eliminations/resolved.sqlc` is the
#    half that lands and this is the whole, and their difference is REPORTED rather than asserted,
#    the way `coupling_search` reports. Writing `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc`'s shape here would
#    accuse the filing the schema names as ordinary.
#
# ⛔ NO FOREIGN KEY, exactly as `composition/part_references.sqlc` has none: a notation may name a
#   document nobody in this corpus filed, and that is a state and not an error.
-- asrt:Elimination/asrt:between, typed asrt:FiledLayer, whose filing is a pm:ForeignId.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, b.layer, b.regime
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/between.sqlc)
) b
